{"type":"assistant","uuid":"1a1cc9eb-3838-4fc7-996a-7d320410cf09","parentUuid":"a3869ac1-f792-4a35-8f35-2741dc432e67","isSidechain":false,"timestamp":"2026-11-09T14:12:10.915Z","sessionId":"718208ce-6423-4004-9771-10cfb6d615e8","session_id":"108d6543-12e9-4ae4-85fb-f303424bfa6a","message":{"id":"msg_011CgsVQjy5hZzmvrsZHcDL7","role":"assistant","model":"claude-haiku-5-5","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tides are the regular rise and fall of sea levels, driven mainly by the gravitational pull of the Moon and, to a lesser extent, the Sun. The Moon's gravity pulls water on the near side of Earth toward it, and the inertia of water on the far side produces a second bulge on the opposite side. As Earth rotates through these two bulges, most coastlines pass through two high tides and two low tides in roughly a day, about 24 hours and 50 minutes. The height of each tide varies with the alignment of the Sun and Moon. At new and full moon, their combined pull produces higher high tides and lower low tides, called spring tides. At the quarter moons, the forces partly cancel, producing smaller ranges called neap tides. Local factors such as basin shape, water depth, and coastline geometry also strongly influence the size of tides at any given place.\n\n1. Gravitational tides: the Moon and Sun pull on ocean water, creating bulges on opposite sides of Earth.\n2. Tidal range: the difference between high and low water, which depends on the Sun–Moon alignment and local geography.\n\n```text\nhigh tide -> low tide -> high tide -> low tide\n```"}],"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":383,"cache_read_input_tokens":45715,"cache_creation_input_tokens":110},"stop_reason":"end_turn"}}
